  "worked_solution": "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,7),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,4,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-6 \\\\  & =-5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-6+9 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(7) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-6+9-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,-5,4,0\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$",
